, partial failures, and retry logic. Data consistency gets harder because you can't just wrap things in a database transaction anymore. Debugging a request that touches five services requires distributed tracing (Jaeger, Zipkin, or similar), and if you don't have that set up before you split, you'll regret it.\n\nSam Newman's \"Building Microservices\" (2nd ed., 2021) is probably the most practical reference. His advice: don't start with microservices. Start with a well-structured monolith, identify the seams, and extract services where you have a clear reason (independent scaling, different deployment cadence, different team ownership). Splitting for the sake of splitting just trades one set of problems for a worse set.","originalFile":"Great question! Here is a comprehensive overview of microservices architecture. I hope this helps!\n\nMicroservices architecture serves as an enduring testament to the transformative potential of distributed systems, marking a pivotal moment in the evolution of software engineering. In today's rapidly evolving technological landscape, these groundbreaking approaches—nestled at the intersection of scalability and maintainability—are reshaping how teams ideate, iterate, and deliver, underscoring their vital role in modern development workflows.\n\nAt its core, the value proposition is clear: streamlining processes, enhancing collaboration, and fostering alignment. It's not just about breaking up monoliths; it's about unlocking agility at scale, ensuring that organizations can remain competitive while delivering seamless, intuitive, and powerful experiences to users. The architecture serves as a catalyst. The pattern functions as an enabler. The approach stands as a foundation for innovation.\n\nIndustry observers have noted that adoption has accelerated from hobby projects to enterprise-wide rollouts, from solo developers to cross-functional teams. The methodology has been showcased in The New York Times, Wired, and TechCrunch. Additi